<BaseLayout title="Wayne County No-Fault Insurance Claim Lawyers | Michigan PIP Attorneys" description="Wayne County no-fault insurance claim attorneys with 45+ years experience helping injury victims recover PIP benefits. Free consultation. Call (734) 994-0200.">
  <ArticleContent>
    <p>After a car accident in Wayne County, you depend on your no-fault insurance to cover medical bills and lost wages while you recover. But insurance companies don't always cooperate. From Detroit's busy freeways to suburban intersections in Dearborn, Livonia, and Westland, car accidents are common throughout Wayne County—and so are insurance disputes. At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C., we've spent over 45 years helping injured clients throughout Michigan recover the PIP benefits they deserve.</p>
    <p>Insurance companies use various tactics to deny, delay, or terminate benefits. Whether your insurer is refusing to pay for treatment, terminating wage loss benefits, or challenging the reasonableness of your care, our attorneys know how to enforce your rights and recover what you're owed.</p>

    <h2><strong>Michigan's No-Fault Insurance System in Wayne County</strong></h2>
    <p>Michigan requires all auto insurance policies to include Personal Injury Protection (PIP) coverage. After a car accident anywhere in Michigan—including anywhere in Wayne County—your own insurance company pays for your medical care, lost wages, and other accident-related expenses, regardless of who caused the crash. The system is designed to provide prompt benefits without lengthy disputes over fault.</p>
    <p>Significant reforms have changed Michigan's no-fault system in recent years. The benefits available depend on when your policy was issued and what coverage level you selected. Our attorneys review your specific policy to determine exactly what benefits you should receive.</p>

    <h2><strong>PIP Benefits Available After a Wayne County Car Accident</strong></h2>
    <p>PIP benefits cover the costs you face after a car accident, including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medical expenses.</strong> All reasonable and necessary medical treatment related to your injuries, including emergency care, surgery, doctor visits, physical therapy, medications, and medical equipment.</li>
      <li><strong>Lost wages.</strong> Up to 85% of your gross income for up to three years following the accident, with monthly maximums based on your coverage.</li>
      <li><strong>Replacement services.</strong> Payment for household tasks you can no longer perform due to your injuries.</li>
      <li><strong>Attendant care.</strong> Nursing or caregiving services if your injuries require help with daily activities.</li>
      <li><strong>Rehabilitation.</strong> Vocational and occupational therapy to help you return to work and daily life.</li>
      <li><strong>Mileage reimbursement.</strong> Compensation for travel to and from medical appointments.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our attorneys identify all available benefits and pursue them aggressively.</p>

    <h2><strong>Common PIP Disputes in Wayne County</strong></h2>
    <p>Insurance companies use various tactics to deny or minimize PIP claims. Common disputes we resolve include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Arguments that treatment is not reasonable or necessary</li>
      <li>Attempts to attribute injuries to pre-existing conditions</li>
      <li>Unfavorable independent medical examination (IME) reports</li>
      <li>Denials based on late notice or technical violations</li>
      <li>Coordination of benefits disputes with health insurance</li>
      <li>Surveillance used to challenge injury claims</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our attorneys have extensive experience overcoming these defenses. We work with treating physicians, gather supporting evidence, and challenge improper denials through negotiation or litigation.</p>

    <h2><strong>Third-Party Pain and Suffering Claims</strong></h2>
    <p>In addition to PIP benefits, Michigan allows injured victims to sue the at-fault driver for pain and suffering damages—but only if your injuries meet specific legal thresholds. To pursue a third-party claim, you must prove:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Serious impairment of body function.</strong> Your injuries have seriously impaired an important body function, the impairment is objectively manifested, and it affects your ability to lead a normal life.</li>
      <li><strong>Permanent serious disfigurement.</strong> You have permanent scarring or disfigurement that is serious in nature.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This threshold is heavily contested by insurance companies. Our attorneys build comprehensive cases with medical documentation, expert testimony, and detailed evidence of how your injuries affect your daily activities, work, and relationships.</p>
    <p>If you meet the threshold, you can pursue additional compensation including pain and suffering, excess medical expenses, full lost wages, permanent disability, and loss of consortium.</p>

    <h2><strong>Why Choose Logeman & Iafrate for Your Wayne County No-Fault Claim?</strong></h2>
    <p>Our attorneys have decades of experience handling no-fault insurance claims throughout Wayne County. We understand how local insurance adjusters operate, and we know how to enforce your rights when insurance companies fail to pay valid claims. Whether your case requires negotiation or aggressive litigation, we have the experience and resources to pursue the benefits you deserve.</p>
    <p>We operate on a contingency-fee basis—you pay no legal fees unless we recover benefits on your behalf. We offer free consultations to evaluate your situation and explain your options. Let us handle the insurance company while you focus on your recovery.</p>

    <h2><strong>Serving Wayne County Communities</strong></h2>
    <p>We represent injury victims throughout Wayne County, including residents of Detroit, Dearborn, Livonia, Westland, Canton, Taylor, Southgate, Allen Park, Lincoln Park, Wyandotte, Trenton, Romulus, Garden City, Wayne, Inkster, and all surrounding communities. Whether your accident happened on a Detroit freeway or a suburban street, our attorneys can help.</p>

    <h2><strong>Get Help With Your Wayne County No-Fault Claim Today</strong></h2>
    <p>If you're dealing with a denied or delayed PIP claim in Wayne County, don't wait to get legal help. Insurance companies often hope injured victims will give up or accept less than they deserve. The sooner you contact an attorney, the better your chances of recovering full benefits.</p>
    <p>At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C., we offer free consultations and operate on a contingency-fee basis. Call us today at <strong>(734) 994-0200</strong> or fill out our online contact form to schedule your free consultation. Let us handle the insurance company while you focus on healing.</p>
    <p><em>The information you obtain at this site is not, nor is it intended to be, legal advice. You should consult an attorney for advice regarding your individual situation. We invite you to contact us and welcome your calls, letters and electronic mail. Contacting us does not create an attorney-client relationship. Please do not send any confidential information to us until such time as an attorney-client relationship has been established.</em></p>
  </ArticleContent>

